## Executive thesis

prompt-egress-guard is strongest when positioned as a local, deterministic prompt-egress control for configured public-AI web use. It is not a full DLP system, not a model gateway, not an endpoint agent, not a compliance platform, and not a universal AI-use monitor. Its value is narrower and more realistic:

It catches risky prompt candidates at the moment a user is about to paste or submit sensitive-looking content into configured public-AI web pages, then blocks, warns, or redirects the user toward an approved AI endpoint while keeping local audit evidence redacted.

That is a credible core-technology wedge because many organizations are trying to support AI adoption without letting employees paste secrets, customer data, health-like records, payroll data, legal-sensitive text, or proprietary source-code-like content into unmanaged external tools. The repo does not try to solve the whole AI governance problem. It provides a practical last-mile control at the browser prompt boundary.

The business value is high when framed as:

- reducing risky prompt egress
- steering work toward approved AI paths
- producing privacy-preserving audit evidence
- supporting AI governance rollout without banning all public AI use
- giving security teams deterministic policy enforcement and reason-code evidence
- giving business teams a way to adopt AI with guardrails instead of vague training alone

The core defensibility comes from the combination of policy schemas, deterministic detector categories, action semantics, browser DOM capture, redacted audit summaries, unsafe policy validation, generated real-world coverage, and extension compilation. The weakest area is production extension runtime wiring and browser deployment maturity. The CLI/core behavior is strong and tested; the extension artifact still needs a clean policy-loading path before it should be represented as production-ready managed-browser enforcement.

